  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry, cool, and breezy today with rough seas. \n\nThe dry and cooler than average weather we've been enjoying for\nthe last several days continues for at least one more day \nthanks to a sprawling high pressure centered over Quebec. \nPlacement of southern New England between this surface high and \nbuilding low pressure to the north will continue the persistent \nand increasingly steady northeast flow keeping temperatures in \nthe low 60s (east) to mid/upper 60s (CT valley). Thanks to those\npersistent winds over a long fetch we'll continue to see \ndangerous seas with waves a high as 12-15 ft over the southern \nouter waters. Rough seas will only increase in magnitude and \narea going into the weekend. Over land, we'll continue to see \nbreezy winds gusting to 25-35 mph. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A strong coastal low pressure system will bring\na long duration of rain, rough seas, strong winds, and coastal\nflooding/erosion Friday through the weekend. Exact magnitude \nand placement of the winds and rain remain uncertain. \n\nBottom line up front: prolonged rain and wind is expected this \nweekend, with the worst of it along the coast. Coastal impacts\nwill also be felt from coastal flooding and erosion, especially \neast facing coastlines. Guidance is ever so slowly coming into \nagreement on the storm's track, leading to still only moderate \nconfidence in exact numbers/times with regard to strongest \nwinds, peak coastal flooding etc. For this reason, watches \ncontinue. However, confidence is high that this will be an \nimpactful early season nor'easter for southern New England as a \ncoastal low meandering off the eastern seaboard is captured by \nanother feature and pulled back to the west after passing near \nthe 70/40 benchmark. Latest GFS guidance seems to be caving \ntoward a more western track in line with the ECMWF/UKMET. It's \nyet to be seen if this is the start of a trend or an outlier, \nbut the 00Z GEFS continues to have quite a wide E-W spread in \nlow centers by Saturday morning. \n\nA western track would have an impact on coastal flooding, \npotentially expanding minor flooding to more southern facing  \ncoastlines toward the later half of the weekend; this is hinted\nat in the Stevens Institute coastal flooding guidance and will\nneed to be monitored as we settle on an eventual low track. \nRegardless, we will no doubt be dealing with a good deal of \ncoastal erosion and at least some minor coastal flooding on the \neast coast, especially south of Boston. This will be a prolonged\ncoastal flooding event from late Fri-Sun which could exacerbate\nflooding concerns as water may not be able to fully evacuate \nbetween high tides. \n\nThe latest ensemble probabilities continue to indicate the\nheaviest rainfall over eastern MA and RI where probabilities \nof 2+\" have increased (80%+) as have probabilities of 4\" \n(40-60%) centered over eastern MA. The slow moving nature of \nthis pattern means that this is spread over 48+ hours which \nshould limit flash flooding concerns. \n\nThe strongest winds will be over the waters where Storm Watches \nremain in effect starting on Friday. On land the strongest winds\nwill be along the coast where the presence of leaves on trees \nwill exacerbate potential tree damage compared to a late season \nstorm. Ensemble guidance indicates >70% chance of gusts over 40 \nmph, while the chance of gusts nearing 60 mph over Cape Cod and \nthe islands has decreased in the last 24 hours for both the EPS \nand GEFS likely owing to the further west track. The low center \nlingers, weakening, Sunday and even into Monday before finally \nexiting east by Tuesday.",
